  Pre-authentication the browser only has what was typed into the form; the account ID is something the server knows and
  returns [3mafter[0m credentials check out. So the change isn't [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m — it's [38;5;153mlogin[39m returning the
  ID and something owning it from there. I'll design against that, but say the word if you're actually working with an
  ID that's known client-side before submit.

  Next question, and it's the one that decides the shape of everything else.

  [1mThe fork:[0m "persist" can mean two unrelated things here, and they need different machinery. Keeping the ID available to
  the rest of the app after login is a [3mclient-side[0m concern. Keeping a record of who logged in and when is a [3mserver-side[0m
  concern. They're independent — you may want one, the other, or both — but which one drives the design changes what we
  build first.

  - [1mClient-side session state[0m — the ID survives page reloads and other forms read it. Buys you "other forms will need it
    later" directly. Costs a storage choice ([38;5;153msessionStorage[39m / [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m / cookie) with real security trade-offs, and
    it's a stored format, so it's expensive to change once other code reads it.
  - [1mServer-side login records[0m — an audit trail of login events. Buys you actual tracking of who logged in. Costs work
    behind [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, which is a stub here — I'd be designing a contract, not an implementation, unless there's a
    real backend.
  - [1mBoth[0m — most likely what you mean, but they get separate sections in the spec and the client-side piece is the one
    that's buildable in this repo today.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m scope this spec to client-side session state, and specify the server contract without implementing
  it. The client half is the part that exists here and the part "other forms will need it later" actually depends on.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Persistence [39m[49m

[1m[38;5;231mWhat does "persist who logged in" need to cover?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mBoth, client first (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mSpec both halves: build client-side session state now, and define the server-side record contract without [39m
     [38;5;246mimplementing it (API_ENDPOINT is a stub in this repo).[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m Client-side session only
     [38;5;246mThe account ID survives reloads and other forms can read it. No server-side audit trail in scope.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m Server-side records only
     [38;5;246mAn audit trail of login events behind the API. The client just sends what it has; no shared client state module.[39m
  [38;5;246m4. Type something.[39m
